   Leonan M. and de Andreazzi, Cecilia Siliansky and Hirata, Daniel Veloso
   and Moratelli, Ricardo},
Title = {Challenges of studying biodiversity in a context of urban conflict},
Journal = {BIOLOGICAL CONSERVATION},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {319},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {Urban biodiversity plays a crucial role in maintaining a healthy
   environment, contributing to human well-being. However, in big cities
   where large forest remnants are adjacent to areas with high levels of
   urban violence, conservation and ecological research face significant
   challenges. Grounded in the experiences of professionals from local
   institutions, this essay discusses the potential barriers imposed by
   armed conflicts and illegal activities in Rio de Janeiro to a full
   understanding of the ecological dynamics of its vast urban forests and
   their consequences. The presence of armed criminal groups in these
   forests poses a threat to personal safety and impairs the conservation
   of these ecosystems by hindering research and restoration activities,
   compromising data collection, and obstructing the implementation of
   conservation guidelines. Additionally, the impediment of on-site
   environmental education and the social perception of these forests as
   unsafe places may reduce community engagement in preservation
   initiatives as well as the usage of these areas for leisure. Finally, we
   raise questions for future research and reflections, which are essential
   for formulating public policies that reconcile security, conservation,
   and the sustainable use of urban ecosystems.},
